      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently working on a site in the Peloponnese and I'm looking at a collection of highly fragmented ceramic shards from a single stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between different firing techniques used in this period. Could you explain the key indicators to look for when trying to differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres in terracotta fragments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When examining terracotta fragments to distinguish between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres, focus on the color and texture of the clay. Oxidized firing typically results in a reddish color and a smooth texture due to the presence of oxygen, whereas reduced firing creates a black or dark color with the clay's mineral structure preserved. You may also look for cracks or crazing in the glaze; reduced firing can cause crazing due to the shrinkage occurring quickly when hydrogen is released in the kiln.",
